avcodec/dca_xll: decode the height channels of the DTS:X standard presentation

A DTS:X stream in the standard presentation is DTS-HD Master Audio with a 7.1
bed and an extension at the end of every XLL frame, behind the syncword the
decoder already recognises (DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X). The extension is a 22-byte
header followed by a regular XLL channel set: four channels, every one coded
in full (no residual against the core), no speaker mask, its own NAVI table
with the frame's segment geometry, then a two-byte trailer. Its channels are
the front and rear height pairs, and the encoder folds each of them into the
bed speaker below it (L, R, Lsr, Rsr) at -3 dB, gain code 23170/32768, so the
7.1 bed plays them on players that ignore the extension.

With the new "x_heights" option the decoder parses that channel set with the
existing channel set code and outputs 7.1.4 (TFL, TFR, TBL, TBR): the bed as
coded, the heights still folded into it, next to the four height channels.
Nothing is taken out of the bed; how a player separates the heights from it
is up to the player. The option is off by default; without it, and for every
stream without the extension, the output is unchanged. A frame whose
extension does not decode keeps the 7.1.4 layout with silent heights, and the
frame still ends where it did before.

The alternate presentations (DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_IMAX and its siblings
0xF14000D1, D3 and D4; FFmpeg labels the first two "DTS:X IMAX") carry the
same four fixed heights after one to five object channels. Their extension
starts with a CRC-protected prefix ended by a marker (a byte naming the
channel sets that follow, then 34 38 8c 4f 00) and a seven- or eight-byte
control block, then the object channel set, then the height channel set, whose
own control block ends right in front of its header with 02 34 38 8c 4f 00.
The outer control block states the height header's position in two-byte
units and both control blocks carry a segment geometry (log2 segments, log2
segment samples, NAVI entry width) for 512 samples. With "x_heights" the
decoder locates the height channel set this way, checking every header and
NAVI table against its CRC, decodes it with that geometry (no LSB split, two
bits where the standard header has its mapping flag) and outputs it like the
standard presentation's heights. The profiles fold their heights into the
bed at different gains (D0 at unity, D1 and D3 at -3 dB, measured in the dca
crate's analysis), so the read-only, exported option "x_presentation" tells a
player which presentation the last decoded heights came from: 1 for the
standard one, else the low byte of the alternate syncword. The prefix is only checked against its CRC
and the object channels are never decoded: they stay where the encoder folded
them, in the 7.1 bed. A stream whose alternate frames do not carry heights
keeps its 7.1.

The format facts (header size, channel order, the fold, the alternate layout)
come from the public analysis in harletty-bridge's dca crate and kristofferR's
SpatialAudioBridge;
this is an implementation on FFmpeg's own XLL code, not a translation.

